Architecture Backend Rules – Full Version

1. Code Style & Conventions
• PSR-12 standard, max 120 characters per line, trailing commas.
• Database: snake_case for tables and columns.
• PHP: PascalCase for classes, camelCase for methods/properties.
• No abbreviations in variable/method names.
• Always import classes via 'use ...' at top of file, never FQCN inline.
• declare(strict_types=1); at the top of every file.
• PHPDoc required for all public methods.
• Forbidden in production: die(), dd(), dump().
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Domain\User\Entity;

use Carbon\Carbon;
use Ramsey\Uuid\Uuid;

final class User
{
   // Example using imported classes instead of \Ramsey\Uuid\Uuid inline
   public static function create(string $name): self
   {
       return new self(Uuid::uuid7()->toString(), $name, Carbon::now());
   }
}

2. Architecture & Structure
• Domain-Driven Design separation: Domain, Application, Infrastructure, API.
• Dependencies only top-down: API -> Application -> Domain.
• Infrastructure provides repository implementations, adapters.
• Patterns: Factory, Strategy, Repository, Observer, DTO.
• Controllers are thin: only HTTP, validation, delegation.
• Application Services orchestrate use-cases.
• Eloquent only in Infrastructure.
3. Technologies & Tools
• Laravel 12, PHP 8.4.
• PostgreSQL as DB, Redis for caching and queues.
• Tests: PHPUnit/Pest for unit/integration, API contract tests, smoke tests.
• Docker Compose for dev. Never commit .env.
• Static analysis with PHPStan/Larastan.
4. Quality Rules
• Follow SOLID, DRY, KISS principles.
• No magic strings – use BackedEnum or Config classes.
• Always use Carbon instead of DateTimeImmutable.
• Avoid complex if/elseif chains – prefer Strategy or maps.
5. Project-Specific Rules
• Translations must always use __('app.key.example').
• Modules are optional vendor packages (e.g., vsc/loyaltykit-invoice).
• Reports and large datasets must always use chunking/streaming.
• Primary keys are UUID, migrations use softDeletesTz().
• Status/type fields always represented by Enums.
6. Prohibitions
• Do not use DB::raw() unless necessary, only in repositories.
• No direct Model::where/create/update outside repositories.
• Do not use facades like DB::, Cache::, Auth::. Prefer dependency injection.
• Do not use global helpers like config() or now(). Use Config classes or Carbon.
7. Git & CI/CD
• Branch naming: feature/*, bugfix/*, hotfix/*.
• Use Conventional Commits (feat:, fix:, refactor:).
• Do not commit vendor, .env, SQL dumps, IDE files.
• CI checks: phpcs, phpstan, tests. Must pass before merge.
• Use composer audit and SAST tools.
8. API Design
• Endpoints must follow REST, responses must follow JSON:API.
• Plural resources: /users, /invoices.
• Single actions use verbs: /users/{id}/block.
• Use Resources/Transformers for responses.
• Swagger/OpenAPI annotations required.
@OA\Post(
 path="/invoices",
 summary="Create invoice",
 tags={"Invoices"},
 @OA\RequestBody(
   required=true,
   @OA\JsonContent(
     required={"number","user_id","net","issued_at"},
     @OA\Property(property="number", type="string"),
     @OA\Property(property="user_id", type="string", format="uuid"),
     @OA\Property(property="net", type="number", format="float"),
     @OA\Property(property="issued_at", type="string", format="date-time")
   )
 ),
 @OA\Response(response=201, description="Created")
)

9. Dependency Injection
• Use Laravel 11+ Attributes for dependency injection.
• Always import via 'use', not fully qualified names.
public function __construct(
   #[DB('pgsql')] Connection $connection,
   #[Cache('redis')] CacheRepository $cache,
   #[Config('services.mail.from.address')] string $from,
   #[Log('daily')] LoggerInterface $logger,
   #[Auth('web')] Guard $guard,
) {}

10. Configuration
• Never call config() directly.
• Use App\Config classes with attributes #[Config(...)] injected.
11. Repositories
• All DB queries must go through repositories.
• Transactions only in repositories.
• Always extend base EloquentRepository.
• Mapping Model <-> Entity must be done in dedicated Mappers.
12. Entities & Enums
• Entities live in Domain only.
• Carbon for datetime.
• Use BackedEnum for status/option fields.
13. DTO & Factories
• Each DTO must have its own Factory.
• DTOs must be readonly, with descriptive field names.
14. Services (Application)
• Only orchestration of use-cases.
• Never open DB transactions directly – delegate to repositories.
• Dispatch long-running tasks to queues with idempotency key.
15. Controllers (API)
• Validation in FormRequest.
• No business logic, only delegation to services.
• DTOs created by Factories, not inline.
• Responses via Resources.
16. API Resources
• Only way of exposing data.
• Resource must not depend on repositories or services.
• All data must be passed in from controller/service.
17. Events & Queues
• Modules decoupled by Events and Listeners.
• Queue jobs must be idempotent.
• Idempotency key stored in DB or cache.
18. Exceptions & Error Handling
• Catch exceptions only to add context or convert type.
• Never swallow exceptions – always rethrow.
• Custom exceptions mapped to correct HTTP codes.
19. Logging & Debugging
• Log only errors/failures – never successes.
• Use injected LoggerInterface.
• Include requestId and context data in logs.
• No dd()/dump() in code.
20. Performance
• Reports must use chunking/streaming.
• Indexes required for filtering/sorting.
• Materialized views for slow-changing datasets.
• Prevent N+1 queries.
21. Migrations & DDL
• Always use UUID primary keys.
• Partial unique indexes with deleted_at IS NULL.
• Foreign keys with ON DELETE/UPDATE where sensible.
• Use softDeletesTz().
22. Routing
• Plural nouns for resources.
• Verbs for single actions.
• No direct queries in views.
23. Vendor Packages
• All optional modules delivered as vendor packages.
• Naming pattern: vsc/<domain>-<feature>.
• Each package must provide config, migrations, service provider.
24. API Resources Example
Example of Resource returning JSON:API shape.

final class InvoiceResource extends JsonResource
{
   public function toArray($request): array
   {
       return [
           'data' => [
               'type' => 'invoices',
               'id'   => $this->resource['id'],
               'attributes' => [
                   'number' => $this->resource['number'],
                   'status' => $this->resource['status'],
               ],
           ],
           'meta' => ['request_id' => app('requestId')],
       ];
   }
}

25. DTO Factory Example
Factories must build DTOs from requests.

final class CreateInvoiceDtoFactory
{
   public function fromRequest(CreateInvoiceRequest $request): CreateInvoiceDto
   {
       return new CreateInvoiceDto(
           invoiceNumber: $request->string('number')->toString(),
           userId: $request->string('user_id')->toString(),
           netAmountCents: (int) round($request->float('net') * 100),
           issuedAt: Carbon::parse($request->string('issued_at')->toString()),
       );
   }
}

26. Service Example
Services orchestrate, no direct DB calls.

final class CreateInvoiceService
{
   public function __construct(
       private InvoiceRepositoryInterface $repository,
       private LoggerInterface $logger,
   ) {}

   public function handle(CreateInvoiceDto $dto): string
   {
       $invoice = Invoice::create(
           $dto->invoiceNumber(),
           $dto->userId(),
           $dto->netAmountCents(),
           $dto->issuedAt(),
       );

       $this->repository->save($invoice);
       event(new InvoiceCreated($invoice->id()));
       return $invoice->id();
   }
}

27. Controller Example
Controllers are thin – no business logic.

final class InvoiceController extends Controller
{
   public function store(
       CreateInvoiceRequest $request,
       CreateInvoiceDtoFactory $dtoFactory,
       CreateInvoiceService $service
   ) {
       $dto = $dtoFactory->fromRequest($request);
       $id = $service->handle($dto);
       return (new InvoiceResource([
           'id' => $id,
           'number' => $dto->invoiceNumber(),
           'status' => 'pending',
       ]))->response()->setStatusCode(201);
   }
}

28. Repository Example
All DB access only in repositories, transactions here.

final class InvoiceRepository extends EloquentRepository implements InvoiceRepositoryInterface
{
   protected string $model = InvoiceModel::class;

   public function save(Invoice $invoice): void
   {
       $this->transactional(function () use ($invoice): void {
           $model = $this->mapper->toModel($invoice);
           $model->saveOrFail();
       });
   }
}

29. Mapper Example
Dedicated mappers for Model ↔ Entity.

final class InvoiceMapper
{
   public function toDomain(InvoiceModel $model): Invoice
   {
       return Invoice::reconstitute(
           $model->id,
           $model->number,
           $model->user_id,
           (int) $model->net_amount_cents,
           Carbon::parse($model->issued_at),
           InvoiceStatus::from($model->status),
       );
   }
}

30. Tests
• Unit: entities, value objects, utils.
• Integration: services + repos, events/listeners.
• API contract tests for JSON:API + OpenAPI.
• E2E smoke tests for critical paths.
31. ADR
• Any deviation from these rules must be recorded as an Architecture Decision Record.
• ADR includes context, options, decision, impact, rollback plan.
32. Definition of Done
• DDD structure applied, no layer violations.
• No abbreviations in names, all imports via use.
• Carbon used instead of DateTimeImmutable.
• All DB queries and transactions in repositories.
• DTOs built only via Factories.
• Resources only output given data, no service/repo dependencies.
• JSON:API + OpenAPI present.
• Only error/failure logs with context.
• All tests green, CI pipeline green.
33. Controllers with __invoke()
When using invokable controllers (__invoke()), do not use __construct for dependency injection. Instead, inject dependencies directly into the __invoke() method.

final class BlockUserController
{
   public function __invoke(
       BlockUserService $service,
       BlockUserDtoFactory $dtoFactory,
       Request $request
   ) {
       $dto = $dtoFactory->fromRequest($request);
       $service->handle($dto);
       return response()->noContent();
   }
}

34. DTO Field Access
All DTO fields must be private and accessible only through explicit getter methods. No public properties are allowed.

final readonly class CreateUserDto
{
   public function __construct(
       private string $firstName,
       private string $lastName,
   ) {}

   public function firstName(): string { return $this->firstName; }
   public function lastName(): string { return $this->lastName; }
}

35. Method Typing
Always add return type declarations to methods. Both return types and parameter types must be strictly declared.

public function handle(CreateUserDto $dto): string
{
   return $dto->firstName().' '.$dto->lastName();
}

36. Custom Exceptions
Never use global exceptions (\Exception, \RuntimeException) directly. Always create dedicated, descriptive exception classes per domain context.

final class UserNotFoundException extends \RuntimeException
{
   public function __construct(string $userId)
   {
       parent::__construct("User with ID {$userId} not found.");
   }
}